"[1803.08601] Design Principles for Sparse Matrix Multiplication on the GPU (https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.08601)\nciteturn5view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.08601\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 174\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 22 Mar 2018 (【5†v1】), last revised 12 Jun 2018 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:Design Principles for Sparse Matrix Multiplication on the GPU\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【6†Carl Yang】, 【7†Aydin Buluc】, 【8†John D. Owens】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Design Principles for Sparse Matrix Multiplication on the GPU, by Carl Yang and 1 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【9†View PDF】 【10†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:We implement two novel algorithms for sparse-matrix dense-matrix multiplication (SpMM) on the GPU. Our algorithms expect the sparse input in the popular compressed-sparse-row (CSR) format and thus do not require expensive format conversion. While previous SpMM work concentrates on thread-level parallelism, we additionally focus on latency hiding with instruction-level parallelism and load-balancing.\nL17: We show, both theoretically and experimentally, that the proposed SpMM is a better fit for the GPU than previous approaches. We identify a key memory access pattern that allows efficient access into both input and output matrices that is crucial to getting excellent performance on SpMM.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - owensgroup/merge-spmm: Code for paper \"Design Principles for Sparse Matrix Multiplication on the GPU\" accepted to Euro-Par 2018 · GitHub (https://github.com/owensgroup/merge-spmm