ource: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn4view0\",\"pattern\":\"sparse\"}); Total lines: 457\nL301: A deserialized verifying key must authenticate its relation and fixed commitments once before verification:\nL302: \nL303:     let mut vk: PoseidonZkVerifyingKey<QuinticExtension> =\nL304:         bincode::deserialize(&vk_bytes)?;\nL305:     vk.authenticate()?;\nL306:     vk.verify(&expected_public_inputs, &proof)?;\nL307: \nL308: DirectSparse authentication validates and hashes the embedded R1CS. Spark authentication also reconstructs the public matrix tables and checks their fixed WHIR commitments.\nL309: #### Circom relation import\nL310: \nL311: `import_r1cs_path` and `import_paths` stream KoalaBear Circom R1CS files into Spartan's sparse matrices; byte import APIs are also available.\nL312: \nL313: #### Linked witness generation\nL314: \nL315: `PoseidonWitnessGenerator::load_library` loads and owns a native witness library; `linked` accepts caller-managed callbacks. See the API documentation for ABI and safety requirements. `prove_from_witness_generator_checked` adds an R1CS satisfaction check for diagnostics.\nL316: #### Relation and transcript binding\nL317: Every `v1` Spartan domain separator binds a canonical SHA-256 digest of the complete padded A, B, and C matrices, their original and padded dimensions, the field and column layout, and the security- and transcript-relevant configuration. Equivalent sparse encodings are combined, normalized, and sorted before hashing.\nL318: This follows the generated-statement mitigation in 【103†How to prove more false statements: Fiat–Shamir limitations on (generated) R1CS†eprint.iacr.org】: the generated R1CS relation is bound before the first Fiat–Shamir challenge.\nL319: The domain separator and expected public inputs are absorbed before the first proof commitment or dependent Fiat-Shamir challenge. Restored-key authentication recomputes the relation digest before accepting serialized key material. An application restricted to one circuit must authorize the complete verifying key, or the relation digest together with every relevant configuration value.\nL320: Poseidon2 transcripts use `spartan-whir-no-zk-v1` and `spartan-whir-full-zk-v1`. Poseidon1 transcripts use `spartan-whir-poseidon1-no-zk-v1` and `spartan-whir-poseidon1-full-zk-v1`. The proof's matrix-closing mode must match the verifying key.\nL321: #### Compact proof transport\nL322: \nL323: `Poseidon1ZkSpartanProtocol::prove_compressed_with_rng` and the corresponding Poseidon2 method produce compact full-ZK Spark proofs. `CompressedZkProofFor::to_bytes` and `from_bytes` implement the `SPC2` encoding, and `verify_compressed` restores derived values before ordinary verification. `ProofCompressionOptions::recommended()` selects the retained compact encoding.\nL324: The authenticated fixed-oracle cache is an optional native transport that omits initial fixed rows and Merkle siblings. It requires a matching authenticated key and retains the fixed codeword and Merkle tree in memory. LeanVM fixtures include their fixed openings directly.\n"
